Baking whole potatoes in an oven usually takes upwards of an hour. With the Instant Pot, high-pressure steam penetrates dense potato starches in a fraction of the time, producing an ultra-fluffy, piping-hot interior that easily separates under a fork. Use this guide and timing chart for standard Russets or sweet potatoes.

## Ingredients

- 4 whole Russet potatoes (or sweet potatoes), scrubbed clean
- 1 cup cold tap water (for 6-quart model; use 1 ½ cups for 8-quart model)
- 1 tablespoon olive oil or melted butter (optional, for finishing)
- Coarse kosher salt and freshly cracked black pepper
- Favorite toppings: salted butter, sour cream, chives, shredded cheese, steamed broccoli, or chili

## Pressure Cooking Timing Chart

All times reflect **HIGH Pressure** followed by a **10-minute Natural Pressure Release (NPR)**:

| Potato Type & Weight | Cook Time (High Pressure) | Release Method |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Small Russet** (approx. 5 oz) | 12 minutes | 10 min Natural Release |
| **Medium Russet** (6 to 7 oz) | 14 minutes | 10 min Natural Release |
| **Large Russet** (8 to 9 oz) | 16 minutes | 10 min Natural Release |
| **Jumbo Russet** (10 to 14 oz) | 18 to 20 minutes | 10 min Natural Release |
| **Small Sweet Potato** (under 8 oz) | 20 minutes | 10 min Natural Release |
| **Medium Sweet Potato** (9 to 14 oz) | 30 to 35 minutes | 10 min Natural Release |
| **Large Sweet Potato** (15+ oz) | 40 to 45 minutes | 10 min Natural Release |

## Instructions

1. **Scrub and Pierce:** Wash the potatoes thoroughly under running water with a vegetable brush to remove surface dirt. Pat dry. Pierce each potato 4 to 5 times with the tines of a fork to prevent pressure pockets from forming under the skin.
2. **Setup the Water & Trivet:** Pour 1 cup of cold water into the inner stainless steel pot of your Instant Pot (use 1 ½ cups if using an 8-quart cooker). Lower the metal steam trivet with handles into the pot.
3. **Load the Potatoes:** Stack the pierced potatoes onto the trivet. It is fine if they touch, but leave a small amount of space around each potato so the steam circulates evenly.
4. **Pressure Cook:** Lock the Instant Pot lid in place and ensure the steam release valve points to **Sealing**. Program on **HIGH Pressure** according to the weight chart above (for average medium 6–7 oz Russets, select **14 minutes**).
5. **Timed Natural Pressure Release:** Once the timer beeps, leave the cooker alone for **10 minutes** of natural pressure release. This resting period allows the trapped heat to finish steaming the interior crumb without rupturing the skins. Afterward, carefully switch the valve to **Venting** to discharge any remaining pressure.
6. **Optional Oven Broil for Crisp Skins:** If you love salty, crisp steakhouse-style potato skins: preheat your oven broiler to HIGH. Transfer the steamed potatoes to a baking sheet. Rub the skins with olive oil, sprinkle generously with coarse kosher salt, and broil 6 inches from the heat element for 3 to 5 minutes until blistered and crisp.
7. **Open and Fluff:** Cut a slit lengthwise down each potato. Press inward on both ends with your fingertips to pop the steaming flesh open. Fluff with a fork, add a generous pat of butter, salt, and pepper, and load with your favorite toppings.

## Notes

- **Uniform Sizing:** Choose potatoes of similar size and weight so they cook at the exact same rate.
- **Batch Size:** You can cook 1 to 6 potatoes simultaneously without changing the cooking time, as long as they fit onto the trivet and do not exceed the Max Fill line.
